          body:
            "Compression of the spinal cord is an emergency because the deficit can become permanent within hours to days. Three groups need it in mind whenever they report back pain:\n\n1. People with cancer, past or present. [[metastatic-spinal-cord-compression|Metastatic spinal cord compression]] happens when a deposit in a vertebra, or tumor growing into the epidural space, presses on the cord or the cauda equina. It can also be the first sign of a cancer not yet diagnosed.\n2. People at risk of [[spinal-epidural-abscess|spinal epidural abscess]]: injection drug use, diabetes, a recent spinal procedure or surgery, vascular catheters or another source of bacteremia, and immune suppression.\n3. People after trauma, including older adults with stiff, degenerate spines after a minor fall.\n\nParalysis is a late sign. Pain usually comes first, often for weeks: new back pain, pain that keeps getting worse, thoracic pain, pain at night or when lying flat, and pain worse on coughing, sneezing, or straining. Pain that wraps around the chest or abdomen in a band, or shoots down a limb, is radicular pain from a compressed root ([[radiculopathy|radiculopathy]]). Then come the neurological warnings: heavy, stiff, or clumsy legs, unsteadiness, numbness or tingling, a [[sensory-level|sensory level]], and early bladder symptoms such as hesitancy, a poor stream, or reduced feeling of fullness. Retention and incontinence are late.\n\nThe examination looks for the level and for sphincter involvement: strength, tone, reflexes, and plantar responses in the legs (and the arms if the neck may be involved); a search for a sensory level with pinprick, moving up from the feet, front and back; saddle sensation and anal tone; and a bladder scan after the patient tries to void. Write the findings down with the time, so that any deterioration is obvious to the next examiner.",
