            "A [[spinal-epidural-abscess|spinal epidural abscess]] is pus in the epidural space. It usually arrives through the bloodstream, most often Staphylococcus aureus, or spreads from an infected disc or vertebra, or follows a spinal procedure. Darouiche describes a typical progression: back pain, then radicular pain, then weakness, sensory loss, and bladder or bowel dysfunction, then paralysis. Fever is often absent, and the classic combination of back pain, fever, and a neurological deficit is present in only a minority, so the diagnosis is often missed at first, especially while the patient is still neurologically normal.\n\nSuspect it in anyone with back pain plus a risk factor, fever, or raised inflammatory markers. Take blood cultures before antibiotics, check the blood count and inflammatory markers (ESR and CRP), and arrange urgent MRI of the whole spine with gadolinium, because abscesses can extend over many segments or appear at separate levels. Avoid lumbar puncture: it adds little and can carry infection into the subarachnoid space.\n\nTreatment is intravenous antibiotics covering staphylococci, including methicillin-resistant strains, and usually gram-negative organisms, with urgent spinal surgical review for drainage, especially when there is a neurological deficit. When the patient has a neurological deficit or is septic, start empiric antibiotics as soon as blood cultures are taken rather than waiting for the scan. Look for the source, including endocarditis in people who inject drugs.",
