          body:
            "Lavy and colleagues separate two stages. In incomplete cauda equina syndrome (CES-I), urinary symptoms are present (altered sensation, loss of the desire to void, a poor stream, or straining) but the patient still has control of the bladder. In cauda equina syndrome with retention (CES-R), there is painless [[urinary-retention|urinary retention]] with overflow, and the bladder is no longer under control. Bladder function is less likely to recover once retention is established, so the aim is to operate at the incomplete stage.\n\nEvery suspected case needs, without delay:\n\n1. A focused examination: pinprick in the saddle area, perianal sensation and anal tone, and leg power, sensation, and reflexes, all documented.\n2. A bladder scan after the patient tries to void.\n3. Emergency MRI of the lumbosacral spine, whatever the time of day.\n4. If MRI shows compression, urgent referral for surgical decompression.\n\nThe clinical diagnosis is unreliable in both directions: Lavy and colleagues quote a false positive rate of 43% for clinical diagnosis even in experienced hands. Many patients scanned for suspected cauda equina syndrome have no compression. That is the right trade, because a missed case can mean permanent incontinence and sexual dysfunction.",
