          body:
            "After a spinal cord injury, the first question is whether any function is preserved in the lowest sacral segments (S4 and S5): sensation at the anal margin, deep anal pressure, or voluntary contraction of the anal sphincter. This [[sacral-sparing|sacral sparing]] defines an incomplete injury under the International Standards for Neurological Classification of Spinal Cord Injury; its absence defines a complete injury, grade A on the [[asia-impairment-scale|ASIA Impairment Scale]]. Incomplete injuries have a much better chance of recovery, which is why the perianal examination matters even when the legs do not move.\n\nIncomplete injuries form patterns that follow from the tract map:\n\n- [[central-cord-syndrome|Central cord syndrome]]: arms weaker than legs, hands worst, with variable sensory loss. Typically an older person with cervical spondylosis after a hyperextension injury, often without a fracture.\n- [[anterior-cord-syndrome|Anterior cord syndrome]]: weakness and loss of pain and temperature below the lesion, with vibration and joint position sense preserved because the [[dorsal-columns|dorsal columns]] are spared. Causes include anterior spinal artery ischemia (for example after aortic surgery) and flexion injuries.\n- [[brown-sequard|Brown-Sequard syndrome]] (hemicord): weakness and loss of vibration and joint position sense on the side of the lesion, and loss of pain and temperature on the opposite side starting a segment or two below. Penetrating injury is the classic cause. This is the crossing logic from the localization module.\n- [[conus-medullaris|Conus]] and [[cauda-equina-syndrome|cauda equina]] injuries give sacral and lower motor neuron patterns, as in the previous lesson.",
