            "[[spinal-shock|Spinal shock]] is a reflex state. After an acute cord injury the limbs below the lesion are flaccid with absent reflexes, and the bladder does not contract. Over days to weeks, reflexes return and upper motor neuron signs appear: [[spasticity|spasticity]], [[hyperreflexia|hyperreflexia]], and a [[babinski-sign|Babinski sign]]. During spinal shock, absent reflexes do not mean the lesion is peripheral; a [[sensory-level|sensory level]] still points to the cord.\n\n[[neurogenic-shock|Neurogenic shock]] is a circulatory state. A cervical or upper thoracic injury cuts the descending sympathetic pathways, so blood vessels dilate, and the vagus nerve slows the heart unopposed. The result is hypotension with bradycardia, or a heart rate that fails to rise, and warm, pink skin. Hemorrhagic shock gives hypotension with tachycardia and cold, pale skin. In a trauma patient, never assume hypotension is neurogenic until bleeding has been looked for; both can be present together.\n\nFirst management is the trauma sequence with the spine protected: immobilize while assessing, support the circulation with fluids and vasopressors, and avoid hypotension and hypoxia, which add secondary injury to the cord. The 2013 guideline from the American Association of Neurological Surgeons and Congress of Neurological Surgeons does not recommend methylprednisolone for acute spinal cord injury.",
